1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,283
Sweetheart, when people walked into my mother's

2
00:00:03,283 --> 00:00:04,540
house in December,

3
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:06,846
the first thing they said wasn't,

4
00:00:06,846 --> 00:00:08,173
what a pretty tree.

5
00:00:08,353 --> 00:00:10,731
It was, oh my goodness,

6
00:00:10,731 --> 00:00:12,695
what is that smell?

7
00:00:12,875 --> 00:00:15,092
Oranges, cinnamon, pine,

8
00:00:15,092 --> 00:00:17,031
and something baking.

9
00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:19,899
Today people spend thirty dollars on a

10
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:22,941
candle that smells like chemicals and gives

11
00:00:22,941 --> 00:00:24,073
them a headache.

12
00:00:24,253 --> 00:00:25,959
My mother did it for about a

13
00:00:25,959 --> 00:00:28,700
quarter, and her house smelled like Christmas

14
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:29,919
from the front walk.

15
00:00:30,268 --> 00:00:33,512
So tonight I'm giving you nine old-fashioned

16
00:00:33,512 --> 00:00:36,092
ways to make your whole house smell

17
00:00:36,092 --> 00:00:38,672
like Christmas, for almost nothing,

18
00:00:38,672 --> 00:00:41,031
and without one chemical candle.

19
00:00:41,211 --> 00:00:42,304
And stay with me,

20
00:00:42,304 --> 00:00:44,425
because number nine is the one my

21
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:46,611
mother did every December morning,

22
00:00:46,611 --> 00:00:48,925
and it's the reason the mailman used

23
00:00:48,925 --> 00:00:50,854
to stand on our porch a little

24
00:00:50,854 --> 00:00:52,461
longer than he needed to.

25
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:54,717
In the nineteen fifties,

26
00:00:54,717 --> 00:00:57,338
honey, nobody had air fresheners.

27
00:00:57,518 --> 00:00:59,437
There were no plug-ins,

28
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:02,023
no fancy candles from the mall.

29
00:01:02,203 --> 00:01:04,322
If you wanted your house to smell

30
00:01:04,322 --> 00:01:06,185
good, you made it smell good,

31
00:01:06,185 --> 00:01:08,240
with what you had in the kitchen

32
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,295
and what you found in the woods.

33
00:01:10,475 --> 00:01:12,853
And those smells are still the ones

34
00:01:12,853 --> 00:01:15,435
that make grown men in their seventies

35
00:01:15,435 --> 00:01:17,134
close their eyes and say,

36
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:18,289
that's Christmas.

37
00:01:18,638 --> 00:01:21,935
Smell is the strongest memory there is,

38
00:01:21,935 --> 00:01:22,864
sweetheart.

39
00:01:23,044 --> 00:01:25,431
Scientists say it goes straight to the

40
00:01:25,431 --> 00:01:27,754
part of the brain that holds memories

41
00:01:27,754 --> 00:01:28,571
and feelings.

42
00:01:28,751 --> 00:01:31,308
That's why one sniff of cinnamon can

43
00:01:31,308 --> 00:01:34,149
put you right back in your grandmother's

44
00:01:34,149 --> 00:01:35,995
kitchen when you were six.

45
00:01:36,175 --> 00:01:38,611
If you want your children to remember

46
00:01:38,611 --> 00:01:40,191
Christmas at your house,

47
00:01:40,191 --> 00:01:42,166
give them a smell to remember.

48
00:01:42,346 --> 00:01:44,591
In a minute I'll tell you which

49
00:01:44,591 --> 00:01:46,328
smell lasts the longest.

50
00:01:46,678 --> 00:01:47,700
Number one.

51
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,030
The simmer pot.

52
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:52,112
Fill a small pot two-thirds with water.

53
00:01:52,292 --> 00:01:54,626
Add the peels of two oranges,

54
00:01:54,626 --> 00:01:56,397
three cinnamon sticks,

55
00:01:56,397 --> 00:01:58,732
a tablespoon of whole cloves,

56
00:01:58,732 --> 00:02:01,308
and a few slices of fresh ginger

57
00:02:01,308 --> 00:02:03,159
or a splash of vanilla.

58
00:02:03,339 --> 00:02:04,821
Bring it to a boil,

59
00:02:04,821 --> 00:02:07,238
then turn it down to the lowest

60
00:02:07,238 --> 00:02:09,734
heat and let it barely steam all

61
00:02:09,734 --> 00:02:10,514
afternoon.

62
00:02:10,694 --> 00:02:13,464
The whole house smells like Christmas in

63
00:02:13,464 --> 00:02:14,572
fifteen minutes.

64
00:02:14,921 --> 00:02:16,963
Just keep an eye on the water,

65
00:02:16,963 --> 00:02:17,371
honey.

66
00:02:17,551 --> 00:02:19,556
Add a cup every hour so it

67
00:02:19,556 --> 00:02:20,789
never boils dry,

68
00:02:20,789 --> 00:02:23,565
because that's how you scorch a pot.

69
00:02:23,745 --> 00:02:25,794
And you can use it for three

70
00:02:25,794 --> 00:02:26,160
days.

71
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,860
Keep it in the icebox overnight and

72
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:30,805
put it back on the stove in

73
00:02:30,805 --> 00:02:31,669
the morning.

74
00:02:31,849 --> 00:02:33,632
When it stops smelling,

75
00:02:33,632 --> 00:02:36,654
throw in a fresh handful of cranberries

76
00:02:36,654 --> 00:02:38,204
and a sprig of pine,

77
00:02:38,204 --> 00:02:40,684
and it comes right back to life.

78
00:02:41,034 --> 00:02:43,810
And here's a Christmas gift idea from

79
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:44,935
the simmer pot.

80
00:02:45,115 --> 00:02:47,707
Put the dry ingredients in a pretty

81
00:02:47,707 --> 00:02:48,447
mason jar.

82
00:02:48,627 --> 00:02:50,347
Dried orange slices,

83
00:02:50,347 --> 00:02:52,238
three cinnamon sticks,

84
00:02:52,238 --> 00:02:54,044
a spoonful of cloves,

85
00:02:54,044 --> 00:02:55,506
a few star anise,

86
00:02:55,506 --> 00:02:58,171
and a little sprig of rosemary.

87
00:02:58,351 --> 00:03:00,390
Tie on a tag that says,

88
00:03:00,390 --> 00:03:02,252
add water and simmer.

89
00:03:02,432 --> 00:03:04,517
I give them to every neighbor,

90
00:03:04,517 --> 00:03:06,879
and they cost about a dollar fifty

91
00:03:06,879 --> 00:03:07,296
a jar.

92
00:03:07,476 --> 00:03:10,309
People love them more than candles.

93
00:03:10,659 --> 00:03:11,774
Number two.

94
00:03:11,954 --> 00:03:14,648
Clove oranges, or pomanders.

95
00:03:14,828 --> 00:03:17,273
My mother made these with Danny and

96
00:03:17,273 --> 00:03:18,531
me every December.

97
00:03:18,711 --> 00:03:21,953
Take an orange and poke whole cloves

98
00:03:21,953 --> 00:03:23,214
into the skin,

99
00:03:23,214 --> 00:03:25,735
all over, in rows or swirls.

100
00:03:25,915 --> 00:03:28,321
Use a toothpick to make the holes

101
00:03:28,321 --> 00:03:30,873
first, or your thumbs will be sore.

102
00:03:31,053 --> 00:03:32,971
Roll it in a little cinnamon,

103
00:03:32,971 --> 00:03:34,756
and tie a ribbon around it.

104
00:03:34,936 --> 00:03:37,344
They smell wonderful for weeks,

105
00:03:37,344 --> 00:03:38,587
and as they dry,

106
00:03:38,587 --> 00:03:40,451
the smell gets stronger.

107
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,280
Hang them from the curtain rods,

108
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,217
put a bowl of them on the

109
00:03:45,217 --> 00:03:47,697
table, or tuck them in a closet.

110
00:03:47,877 --> 00:03:50,633
The old ones from the year before,

111
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:52,173
all dried and hard,

112
00:03:52,173 --> 00:03:55,010
went in our dresser drawers to make

113
00:03:55,010 --> 00:03:56,956
the sweaters smell good.

114
00:03:57,136 --> 00:03:59,203
Grandma Pearl said in her day,

115
00:03:59,203 --> 00:04:01,891
people carried them in their pockets to

116
00:04:01,891 --> 00:04:04,097
church, to cover up the smell of

117
00:04:04,097 --> 00:04:06,853
the farmers who'd just finished milking.

118
00:04:07,203 --> 00:04:08,643
Number three.

119
00:04:08,823 --> 00:04:09,996
Real greenery.

120
00:04:10,176 --> 00:04:12,133
A fake tree looks fine,

121
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:14,855
but it smells like a plastic box

122
00:04:14,855 --> 00:04:16,132
from the attic.

123
00:04:16,312 --> 00:04:18,654
Even if you keep your fake tree,

124
00:04:18,654 --> 00:04:20,631
tuck real branches into it.

125
00:04:20,811 --> 00:04:23,016
Most tree lots will give you their

126
00:04:23,016 --> 00:04:25,675
trimmed-off branches for free if you ask.

127
00:04:25,855 --> 00:04:28,467
Fir, cedar, pine.

128
00:04:28,647 --> 00:04:30,095
Put some on the mantel,

129
00:04:30,095 --> 00:04:31,229
over the doorways,

130
00:04:31,229 --> 00:04:33,117
in a jug on the kitchen table.

131
00:04:33,297 --> 00:04:36,596
Cedar smells the strongest and lasts the

132
00:04:36,596 --> 00:04:37,256
longest.

133
00:04:37,606 --> 00:04:39,445
If this is helping you,

134
00:04:39,445 --> 00:04:41,603
tap the like button for me,

135
00:04:41,603 --> 00:04:42,483
sweetheart.

136
00:04:42,663 --> 00:04:45,565
It helps Grandma more than you know.

137
00:04:45,745 --> 00:04:47,811
And here's a trick for when the

138
00:04:47,811 --> 00:04:49,344
branches start to fade.

139
00:04:49,524 --> 00:04:51,772
Mist them with a spray bottle of

140
00:04:51,772 --> 00:04:53,669
water every couple of days.

141
00:04:53,849 --> 00:04:56,693
It brings the smell right back out.

142
00:04:56,873 --> 00:04:59,024
And if you have a fireplace,

143
00:04:59,024 --> 00:05:01,558
toss a little handful of dry pine

144
00:05:01,558 --> 00:05:03,247
needles into the fire.

145
00:05:03,427 --> 00:05:06,376
It crackles and smells like the woods.

146
00:05:06,726 --> 00:05:09,133
And remember I promised to tell you

147
00:05:09,133 --> 00:05:11,196
which smell lasts the longest?

148
00:05:11,376 --> 00:05:13,176
It's cedar, sweetheart.

149
00:05:13,356 --> 00:05:16,240
A few cedar branches will smell good

150
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:17,521
for three weeks,

151
00:05:17,521 --> 00:05:20,085
long after the pine and fir have

152
00:05:20,085 --> 00:05:20,566
faded.

153
00:05:20,746 --> 00:05:23,636
Grandma Pearl lined her whole front porch

154
00:05:23,636 --> 00:05:26,033
railing with cedar every December,

155
00:05:26,033 --> 00:05:28,289
and in January she put the dried

156
00:05:28,289 --> 00:05:30,756
branches in the closets to keep the

157
00:05:30,756 --> 00:05:31,531
moths away.

158
00:05:31,711 --> 00:05:34,335
Nothing went to waste in her house.

159
00:05:34,685 --> 00:05:35,904
Number four.

160
00:05:36,084 --> 00:05:37,861
Cinnamon pinecones.

161
00:05:38,041 --> 00:05:40,228
Gather pinecones from outside,

162
00:05:40,228 --> 00:05:42,270
dry ones, and bake them on a

163
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:45,113
cookie sheet at two hundred degrees for

164
00:05:45,113 --> 00:05:47,009
half an hour to get rid of

165
00:05:47,009 --> 00:05:47,665
any bugs.

166
00:05:47,845 --> 00:05:49,820
Then put them in a paper bag

167
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:52,147
with a few drops of cinnamon oil,

168
00:05:52,147 --> 00:05:55,038
or sprinkle them with ground cinnamon and

169
00:05:55,038 --> 00:05:55,531
cloves.

170
00:05:55,711 --> 00:05:57,522
Close the bag for a week.

171
00:05:57,702 --> 00:05:59,766
Then put them in a basket by

172
00:05:59,766 --> 00:06:00,872
the front door.

173
00:06:01,052 --> 00:06:03,142
Every guest gets a sniff.

174
00:06:03,491 --> 00:06:05,469
So far, a simmer pot,

175
00:06:05,469 --> 00:06:08,199
clove oranges, real greenery,

176
00:06:08,199 --> 00:06:10,365
and cinnamon pinecones.

177
00:06:10,545 --> 00:06:11,880
Number five.

178
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:13,418
Something baking.

179
00:06:13,598 --> 00:06:15,554
Nothing in the world makes a house

180
00:06:15,554 --> 00:06:17,510
smell like home more than bread or

181
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:18,660
cookies in the oven.

182
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,457
But you don't have to bake a

183
00:06:20,457 --> 00:06:21,150
whole batch.

184
00:06:21,330 --> 00:06:23,381
Keep a roll of cookie dough in

185
00:06:23,381 --> 00:06:25,979
the freezer and bake six cookies right

186
00:06:25,979 --> 00:06:27,414
before company comes.

187
00:06:27,594 --> 00:06:29,519
Or put a pan of cinnamon and

188
00:06:29,519 --> 00:06:31,444
sugar in a warm oven for ten

189
00:06:31,444 --> 00:06:31,994
minutes.

190
00:06:32,344 --> 00:06:34,938
My mother kept a jar of cookie

191
00:06:34,938 --> 00:06:37,791
dough in the icebox all December,

192
00:06:37,791 --> 00:06:40,471
honey, for exactly this reason.

193
00:06:40,651 --> 00:06:42,774
When the doorbell rang and it was

194
00:06:42,774 --> 00:06:44,767
the minister, or the neighbors,

195
00:06:44,767 --> 00:06:46,889
she'd have a tray of warm cookies

196
00:06:46,889 --> 00:06:48,883
on the table in twelve minutes.

197
00:06:49,063 --> 00:06:51,530
And the whole house would smell like

198
00:06:51,530 --> 00:06:53,312
she'd been baking all day.

199
00:06:53,492 --> 00:06:56,496
People thought she was the best housekeeper

200
00:06:56,496 --> 00:06:57,474
on Elm Street.

201
00:06:57,654 --> 00:06:59,407
She just knew the trick.

202
00:06:59,757 --> 00:07:01,093
Number six.

203
00:07:01,273 --> 00:07:02,855
The light bulb trick,

204
00:07:02,855 --> 00:07:04,663
from my mother's sister.

205
00:07:04,843 --> 00:07:05,669
In her day,

206
00:07:05,669 --> 00:07:07,996
people put a drop of vanilla on

207
00:07:07,996 --> 00:07:09,347
a cold light bulb.

208
00:07:09,527 --> 00:07:11,278
Don't do that today,

209
00:07:11,278 --> 00:07:11,803
honey.

210
00:07:11,983 --> 00:07:14,539
Instead, put a few drops of vanilla

211
00:07:14,539 --> 00:07:16,877
or cinnamon oil on a cotton ball

212
00:07:16,877 --> 00:07:19,652
and tuck it into the lampshade's base,

213
00:07:19,652 --> 00:07:22,355
near the warm lamp but never touching

214
00:07:22,355 --> 00:07:23,012
the bulb.

215
00:07:23,192 --> 00:07:25,792
The gentle warmth carries the smell through

216
00:07:25,792 --> 00:07:26,699
the whole room.

217
00:07:27,048 --> 00:07:28,395
Number seven.

218
00:07:28,575 --> 00:07:30,665
A pot of coffee with a cinnamon

219
00:07:30,665 --> 00:07:32,081
stick in the grounds.

220
00:07:32,261 --> 00:07:33,436
Before you brew,

221
00:07:33,436 --> 00:07:35,418
put a whole cinnamon stick,

222
00:07:35,418 --> 00:07:38,207
or half a teaspoon of ground cinnamon,

223
00:07:38,207 --> 00:07:40,776
right in the coffee filter with the

224
00:07:40,776 --> 00:07:41,364
grounds.

225
00:07:41,544 --> 00:07:43,867
The kitchen smells like a bakery,

226
00:07:43,867 --> 00:07:47,035
and the coffee tastes like Christmas morning.

227
00:07:47,215 --> 00:07:49,391
My father drank it that way from

228
00:07:49,391 --> 00:07:52,111
Thanksgiving to New Year's and called it

229
00:07:52,111 --> 00:07:53,403
his holiday coffee.

230
00:07:53,753 --> 00:07:54,624
Number eight.

231
00:07:54,804 --> 00:07:56,801
Start with a clean smell.

232
00:07:56,981 --> 00:07:59,531
All the cinnamon in the world won't

233
00:07:59,531 --> 00:08:01,207
cover up a stale house.

234
00:08:01,387 --> 00:08:04,042
Open the windows for ten minutes in

235
00:08:04,042 --> 00:08:06,318
the morning, even in the cold,

236
00:08:06,318 --> 00:08:08,214
to let fresh air through.

237
00:08:08,394 --> 00:08:10,879
Sprinkle baking soda on the rugs,

238
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:12,385
wait twenty minutes,

239
00:08:12,385 --> 00:08:13,665
and vacuum it up.

240
00:08:13,845 --> 00:08:16,457
Wipe the kitchen counters with a little

241
00:08:16,457 --> 00:08:18,600
white vinegar and a few drops of

242
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:19,336
orange oil.

243
00:08:19,516 --> 00:08:22,303
Then add your Christmas smells on top.

244
00:08:22,652 --> 00:08:23,777
And number nine,

245
00:08:23,777 --> 00:08:25,392
the one I promised you.

246
00:08:25,572 --> 00:08:27,412
Every December morning,

247
00:08:27,412 --> 00:08:29,572
before anybody else was up,

248
00:08:29,572 --> 00:08:32,693
my mother put orange peels and cinnamon

249
00:08:32,693 --> 00:08:34,693
on top of the warm stove,

250
00:08:34,693 --> 00:08:36,213
right on the metal,

251
00:08:36,213 --> 00:08:39,013
and let them toast slowly while she

252
00:08:39,013 --> 00:08:40,213
made breakfast.

253
00:08:40,393 --> 00:08:42,930
Today, honey, put them in a dry

254
00:08:42,930 --> 00:08:45,549
cast iron pan on the lowest heat

255
00:08:45,549 --> 00:08:46,941
for five minutes.

256
00:08:47,121 --> 00:08:49,592
The smell of toasted orange peel goes

257
00:08:49,592 --> 00:08:51,730
through every room in the house.

258
00:08:52,079 --> 00:08:54,076
The mailman, Mr.

259
00:08:54,256 --> 00:08:57,127
Bowers, used to stand on our front

260
00:08:57,127 --> 00:08:59,828
porch with our mail in his hand,

261
00:08:59,828 --> 00:09:01,095
just breathing.

262
00:09:01,275 --> 00:09:03,728
My mother would open the door and

263
00:09:03,728 --> 00:09:05,809
say, would you like a cup of

264
00:09:05,809 --> 00:09:06,627
coffee, Mr.

265
00:09:06,807 --> 00:09:07,898
Bowers?

266
00:09:08,078 --> 00:09:10,272
And he always said yes.

267
00:09:10,452 --> 00:09:13,169
Twenty-two years he delivered our mail,

268
00:09:13,169 --> 00:09:14,562
and every Christmas,

269
00:09:14,562 --> 00:09:16,652
he got a cup of holiday coffee

270
00:09:16,652 --> 00:09:17,558
on our porch.

271
00:09:17,738 --> 00:09:19,810
He came to her funeral,

272
00:09:19,810 --> 00:09:20,350
honey.

273
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:22,132
He told that story.

274
00:09:22,482 --> 00:09:24,131
So one more time.

275
00:09:24,311 --> 00:09:26,401
A simmer pot on the stove.

276
00:09:26,581 --> 00:09:28,055
Clove oranges.

277
00:09:28,235 --> 00:09:30,534
Real greenery, misted.

278
00:09:30,714 --> 00:09:32,966
Cinnamon pinecones by the door.

279
00:09:33,146 --> 00:09:34,818
Something baking.

280
00:09:34,998 --> 00:09:37,332
A drop of vanilla near a lamp.

281
00:09:37,512 --> 00:09:39,207
Cinnamon in the coffee.

282
00:09:39,387 --> 00:09:41,256
A clean house first.

283
00:09:41,436 --> 00:09:44,281
And toasted orange peel every morning.

284
00:09:44,461 --> 00:09:46,956
That's how my mother's house smelled,

285
00:09:46,956 --> 00:09:48,777
and if you close your eyes,

286
00:09:48,777 --> 00:09:50,800
I'll bet you can smell it too.

287
